<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerardo Barrios</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerardo Barrios was a Salvadoran general and liberal president from 1859 to 1863, a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a>'s dream of Central American union. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> cultivation, education and a professional army, and clashed with the Church. Overthrown in 1863 by the Guatemalan forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a>, he was captured after a failed attempt to return and executed in 1865.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios.jpg" alt="Gerardo Barrios" width="300" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Italo Lopez Vallecillos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gerardo_Barrios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Barrios was born in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, either at Cacahuatique, now Ciudad Barrios, or at La Poza de la Juana; the exact day is disputed. His paternal grandfather, Pedro Joaquín Barrios, and a family friend taught him geography, history, mathematics, astronomy and physics and gave him his liberal ideals. He limped throughout his life. In 1843 he married Adelaida Guzmán Saldós, daughter of the future president Joaquín Eufrasio Guzmán; they had no children.</p><p>From 1828, while still a teenager, he fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> in the civil wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>, and he sat in its Federal Congress from 1836 to 1838. He helped overthrow President Francisco Malespín in 1844 and 1845, fought at the Salvadoran defeat at La Arada on 2 February 1851, and commanded a division in the Filibuster War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a>. In June 1857 he attempted a coup against President Rafael Campo and surrendered after a week.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Barrios served twice as acting president before winning the election of 1859 unopposed and taking office on 1 February 1860. He set out to promote agriculture and trade, attract immigrants, reform education and build roads and ports. He rebuilt the Asunción College on secular lines and founded normal schools at San Miguel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> and Santa Ana. He encouraged the transfer of state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> planters, and the coffee oligarchs gained a power that lasted until 1931. When he ordered all priests to swear loyalty to the constitution in October 1861, Archbishop Tomás Miguel Pineda y <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saldana">Saldaña</a> and many priests left the country; a concordat with the Holy See followed in April 1862.</p><p>Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, who had first supported him, broke down over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. Barrios beat the Guatemalans at Coatepeque in February 1863, but after a long siege Carrera took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 26 October 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, New York City and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, Barrios published a manifesto in 1864 calling the new government usurpers. In 1865 he sailed with 800 rifles on the Manuela Planas, but lightning damaged the ship and it put in at El Realejo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, where he was seized and then extradited. Court-martialled from 10 August, he was shot on 29 August 1865, five hours after the sentence; the New York Times called it a barbarous act. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> declared him a national hero in 1910.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerardo Barrios” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Surrounded by larger neighbours, it remained for decades entangled in wars to restore or prevent the union, and several of its leaders, such as <b>Gerardo Barrios</b>, saw themselves as heirs of Morazán’s federal project.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nuevo Edén de San Juan, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.83,-88.49&amp;year=1846" aria-label="Nuevo Edén de San Juan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2126 1649 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gerardo Barrios"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2576 1919h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2576 1919h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.83,-88.49&amp;year=1846">What was here in 1846? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
